two lynchings and the city's first sit-in during the Civil Rights Movement."}, "D2S4": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0407-dc7e1db5c944:d01", "start": 341, "end": 454, "text": "Five years after it was opened, the building was almost destroyed by mob violence in the Omaha Race Riot of 1919."}, "D2S5": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0407-dc7e1db5c944:d01", "start": 455, "end": 564, "text": "The 1895 building was designed in the French Renaissance Revival style by local architect John Latenser, Sr.."}, "D2S6": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0407-dc7e1db5c944:d01", "start": 565, "end": 636, "text": "Decorative stonework covers the structure's exterior, and the building."}, "D3S1": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0407-dc7e1db5c944:d02", "start": 0, "end": 215, "text": "Douglas County Courthouse (Wisconsin) The Douglas County Courthouse is an historic building located at 1313 Belknap Street in Superior, Wisconsin which was listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 1982."}, "D3S2": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0407-dc7e1db5c944:d02", "start": 216, "end": 336, "text": "Designed by Royer and Radcliff in the Classical Revival style, it was built in 1918-1919 of Bedford limestone and brick."}, "D3S3": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0407-dc7e1db5c944:d02", "start": 337, "end": 392, "text": "The building has a colossal Ionic ten-column colonnade."}, "D3S4": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0407-dc7e1db5c944:d02", "start": 393, "end": 508, "text": "At the center of the interior is a three-story light court, with balconies on massive concrete, marble-faced piers."}, "D3S5": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0407-dc7e1db5c944:d02", "start": 509, "end": 608, "text": "The listing includes a jail and sheriff's residence, which is a brick building with cut stone trim."}, "D3S6": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0407-dc7e1db5c944:d02", "start": 609, "end": 629, "text": "It is connected by a"}, "D4S1": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0407-dc7e1db5c944:d03", "start": 0, "end": 100, "text": "Radcliff in the Classical Revival style , it was built in 1918-1919 of Bedford limestone and brick ."}, "D4S2": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0407-dc7e1db5c944:d03", "start": 101, "end": 157, "text": "The building has a colossal Ionic ten-column colonnade ."}, "D4S3": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0407-dc7e1db5c944:d03", "start": 158, "end": 282, "text": "[ 2 ] At the center of the interior is a three-story light court , with balconies on massive concrete , marble-faced piers ."}, "D4S4": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0407-dc7e1db5c944:d03", "start": 283, "end": 391, "text": "[ 2 ] The listing includes a jail and sheriff 's residence , which is a brick building with cut stone trim ."}, "D4S5": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0407-dc7e1db5c944:d03", "start": 392, "end": 453, "text": "It is connected by a second-story walkway to the courthouse ."}, "D4S6": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0407-dc7e1db5c944:d03", "start": 454, "end": 525, "text": "[ 2 ] References [ edit ] ^ a b `` National Register Information System"}, "D5S1": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0407-dc7e1db5c944:d04", "start": 0, "end": 163, "text": "Douglas County Courthouse (Illinois) The Douglas County Courthouse is a government building in Tuscola, the county seat of Douglas County, Illinois, United States."}, "D5S2": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0407-dc7e1db5c944:d04", "start": 164, "end": 243, "text": "Completed in 1913, it is the third courthouse in the history of Douglas County."}, "D5S3": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0407-dc7e1db5c944:d04", "start": 244, "end": 351, "text": "Douglas County's first pioneers arrived in 1829, although occasional squatters had been present beforehand."}, "D5S4": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0407-dc7e1db5c944:d04", "start": 352, "end": 544, "text": "The widespread prairies were deemed a barrier to early settlement, so the population of the area (then part of Coles County) remained low into the 1850s, and Douglas County was formed in 1859."}, "D5S5": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0407-dc7e1db5c944:d04", "start": 545, "end": 633, "text": "The bill providing for the creation of the county permitted an April 1859 public vote on"}, "D6S1": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0407-dc7e1db5c944:d05", "start": 0, "end": 49, "text": "get a kick out of the Douglas County Courthouse ."}, "D6S2": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0407-dc7e1db5c944:d05", "start": 50, "end": 157, "text": "As long as we all pick up after ourselves , I think it ’ ll continue to serve for another century or more ."}, "D6S3": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0407-dc7e1db5c944:d05", "start": 158, "end": 188, "text": "TL ; DR Douglas County ( pop ."}, "D6S4": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0407-dc7e1db5c944:d05", "start": 189, "end": 222, "text": "19,714 , 58/102 ) Tuscola ( pop ."}, "D6S5": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0407-dc7e1db5c944:d05", "start": 223, "end": 325, "text": "4,650 ) 6/102 photographed Built : 1913 Cost : $ 170,000 ( $ 5.4 million today ) Architect : Joseph W."}, "D6S6": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0407-dc7e1db5c944:d05", "start": 326, "end": 506, "text": "Royer Style : Neoclassical Courthouse Square : Shelbyville Square Height : 2.5 stories Current Use : County offices and courts Photographed : 11/7/2020 Sources Cited 1 Gannett , H."}, "D7S1": {"document_number": 7, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0407-dc7e1db5c944:d06", "start": 0, "end": 162, "text": "significant example of the French Renaissance Revival style , and marked a professional milestone in the career trajectory of its architect , John Latenser , Sr ."}, "D7S2": {"document_number": 7, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0407-dc7e1db5c944:d06", "start": 163, "end": 230, "text": "The current Douglas County is the third in the county ’ s history ."}, "D7S3": {"document_number": 7, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0407-dc7e1db5c944:d06", "start": 231, "end": 345, "text": "The first was opened in 1858 and , though found to be too small only a decade later , remained in use until 1885 ."}, "D7S4": {"document_number": 7, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0407-dc7e1db5c944:d06", "start": 346, "end": 516, "text": "The second courthouse was used until the current one was finished in 1912 , when it was then torn down with some materials being reused in various projects around Omaha ."}, "D7S5": {"document_number": 7, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0407-dc7e1db5c944:d06", "start": 517, "end": 527, "text": "In 1908 it"}, "D8S1": {"document_number": 8, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0407-dc7e1db5c944:d07", "start": 0, "end": 543, "text": "14 ] Clay † Louisville 38°46′20″N 88°30′6″W ﻿ / ﻿ 38.77222°N 88.50167°W ﻿ / 38.77222 ; -88.50167 ﻿ ( Clay County Courthouse ) 1912 [ 2 ] : 31 Neoclassical structure , built under the guise of remodelling the previous courthouse ; [ 2 ] : 31 not substantially modified from original construction [ 15 ] Clinton Carlyle 38°36′39″N 89°22′6″W ﻿ / ﻿ 38.61083°N 89.36833°W ﻿ / 38.61083 ; -89.36833 ﻿ ( Clinton County Courthouse ) 1999 [ 2 ] : 32 Replaced the 150-year-old previous courthouse [ 2 ] : 32 Coles † Charleston 39°29′42″N 88°10′30″W ﻿ / ﻿"}}, "original_cards": [{"doc_id": "ramdocs-0407-dc7e1db5c944:d00", "card_id": "ramdocs-0407-dc7e1db5c944:d00:c0", "entity": "Douglas County Courthouse", "scope": "Construction date", "claim": "The Douglas County Courthouse was built in 1912.", "answer": "1912", "quote": "Built in 1912, it was added to the National Register of Historic Places in 1979.", "original_document_number": 1}, {"doc_id": "ramdocs-0407-dc7e1db5c944:d01", "card_id": "ramdocs-0407-dc7e1db5c944:d01:c0", "entity": "Douglas County Courthouse", "scope": "Nebraska", "claim": "The present Douglas County Courthouse was built in 1895.", "answer": "1895", "quote": "Built in 1895, it was added to the National Register o